course: english
lesson: 32
title: Active and passive voice, modal verbs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In the active voice the doer comes first: "The cat chased the mouse." In the passive voice the thing the action happens to comes first: "The mouse was chased by the cat."
explain: The passive is built from a form of "be" plus a past form like chased, eaten or broken, and the doer can be added with "by", or left out altogether.
explain: Leaving the doer out is handy and sometimes sneaky: a science report says "The water was heated" because who did it doesn't matter, but when Mum says "The biscuits have been eaten", every child in the kitchen goes quiet.
explain: Modal verbs such as can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should and must sit in front of another verb and change how sure, how able or how bossy a sentence is: "You must tidy up" orders, "You could tidy up" gently suggests.
explain: Dialects have their own forms: Irish English "The window got broke" and Scots "Ye cannae dae that" ("cannae" is can't, "dae" is do) are real grammar of real places; in formal writing they become "The window was broken" and "You can't do that".
fact: In the active voice the subject does the action: "The dog ate the cake."
fact: In the passive voice the subject receives the action: "The cake was eaten by the dog."
fact: The passive uses a form of "be" (is, was, were, has been) plus a past participle such as eaten, thrown or broken.
fact: When you turn active into passive, the tense stays the same: "ate" becomes "was eaten", "eats" becomes "is eaten".
fact: In a passive sentence the doer can be added with "by ..." or left out completely: "The window was broken."
fact: The passive is useful when the doer is unknown or does not matter, which is why science reports often use it.
fact: The passive can also hide who is responsible, as in "Mistakes were made."
fact: "Got" passives like "The window got broke" are informal or dialect; Standard English writes "The window was broken" or, less formally, "got broken".
fact: Modal verbs (can, could, may, might, will, would, shall, should, must) help a main verb and show certainty, ability, permission or duty.
fact: "Will" sounds sure, "might" sounds unsure; "must" is strong and bossy, "could" is gentle and polite.
fact: In rules and formal notices "shall" works like "must": "Bins shall be put out on Tuesday."
fact: "Could have" ("could've") plus a past form, as in "he could have helped", means he was able to but didn't.
fact: "Can I?" asks if you are able or allowed; "May I?" asks permission more politely and formally.
fact: In Scotland "cannae" means can't, "dinnae" means don't, and "wee" means little.
fact: Very formal writing says "by whom?"; in everyday talk "by who?" is normal.
exercise: Which sentence is active? | The cat chased the mouse. | The mouse was chased by the cat. | The mouse was chased. | The mouse had been chased.
exercise: In "The mouse was chased by the cat", who did the chasing? | The cat | The mouse | Nobody, it chased itself | We cannot tell at all
exercise: Year 6 test: rewrite "The dog ate the cake" in the passive. | The cake was eaten by the dog. | The cake is eaten by the dog. | The dog was eaten by the cake. | The cake ate the dog.
exercise: Which two parts build a passive verb? | A form of "be" and a past participle | A modal verb and a word ending in -ing | "Have" and the word "by" | A noun and an adjective
exercise: Mum says "Mistakes were made." Ruby asks "By who?" What has Ruby noticed? | The passive hides who did it | The sentence has no verb in it | Mum used the future tense | The sentence is a question
exercise: Aoife in Dublin says "The window got broke, so it did." Which is the formal written form? | The window was broken. | The window got broked. | The window broke itself. | The window been broken.
exercise: Which is the more polite request? | You could tidy up. | You must tidy up. | Tidy up now. | You will tidy up.
exercise: Leo says "I might go" and his mate says "I will go". Who is more sure? | His mate | Leo | They are both exactly as sure | Nobody, because modals don't show it
exercise: Tom from Glasgow says "Ye cannae dae that, ya numpty." ("Numpty" means a silly person.) What is the standard? | You can't do that. | You can do that. | You shouldn't have done that yesterday. | You don't do that.
exercise: A science report says "The water was heated." Max asks "Who heated it?" Why is the passive used? | The action matters more than the person | The scientist forgot who did it | Passives are the only verbs used in science | It makes the report shorter and funnier
exercise: Mum, dead dry: "The biscuits have been eaten." Josh: "Not by me." Why does Josh feel accused? | The passive left the eater a mystery | Mum used the word "Josh" in it | It was in the future tense | "Biscuits" is an active word
exercise: Which sentence is passive? A) The ball was thrown. B) The boy threw the ball. | A | B | Both | Neither
exercise: Which list contains only modal verbs? | can, might, should, must | can, run, should, happy | might, quickly, must, and | ball, shall, will, wall
exercise: At the dentist, which sounds more formal? | May I come in? | Can I come in, yeah? | Lemme in, innit. | I'm coming in.
exercise: A wee boy in Edinburgh says "I dinnae want ma tea." ("Wee" means little.) What does "dinnae" mean? | Don't | Didn't ever | Always | Never again
exercise: Which passive has the doer left out? | The vase was smashed. | The vase was smashed by Ben. | Ben smashed the vase. | Ben was smashing the vase.
test: Your mate goes "passive is lazy, teachers hate it, no cap." ("No cap" means for real.) What is true? | It is useful when the doer is unknown or not important | It is always wrong and loses marks in every test | It is only used in poems about the past | It means the sentence has no verb at all
test: ★★★ "The cake was eaten." How many people could have eaten it, from the sentence alone? | We cannot tell | Exactly one | Exactly two | Nobody
test: ★★★ Turn into the active: "The trophy was won by our class." | Our class won the trophy. | The trophy won our class. | Our class was won by the trophy. | Our class wins the trophy.
test: In a cartoon, a villain says "The bank has been robbed... by someone!" Why is it funny? | The passive lets him hide that it was him | He uses the wrong tense on purpose | He forgot how to say "bank" properly | Villains are not allowed to use "by"
test: What would happen if Mum turned "You must go to bed" into "You might go to bed"? | It would turn an order into a maybe | It would mean the same thing exactly | It would make it a stronger order | It would change it to the past tense
test: Gen Alpha cousin: "lowkey the window got smashed, it wasn't me fr." ("fr" means for real, "lowkey" means a bit secretly.) What is the formal passive? | The window was smashed. | The window has smashing. | The window smashed by me. | The window were smash.
test: A gran in Cornwall: "Ee could've 'elped, my lover." ("My lover" is a friendly word there.) What does "could have helped" suggest? | He was able to help but didn't | He definitely helped yesterday | He will help next week | He is helping right now
test: ★★★ Which change does NOT keep the same tense? | "The dog eats the bone" becomes "The bone was eaten by the dog" | "The dog ate the bone" becomes "The bone was eaten by the dog" | "The dog eats the bone" becomes "The bone is eaten by the dog" | "The dog has eaten the bone" becomes "The bone has been eaten by the dog"
test: Ellie in Texas: "Y'all should've told me!" ("Y'all" means you all.) Which modal is she using? | should | told | me | all
test: Which sentence is the strongest order? | You must leave now. | You might leave now. | You could leave now. | You may leave now.
test: Formal letter from the council: "Bins shall be put out on Tuesday." What does "shall" do here? | It states a rule, like "must" | It asks a polite question | It shows a guess | It shows the past
exam: Identify the sentence composed in the passive voice. | The letter was posted by Grandad. | Grandad posted the letter. | Grandad is posting the letter. | Grandad will post the letter.
exam: Ascertain the passive form, in the identical tense, of the sentence "The dog ate the cake". | The cake was eaten by the dog. | The cake is eaten by the dog. | The cake will be eaten by the dog. | The cake has been eating by the dog.
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the principal reason for the use of the passive in scientific reports? | The action is of greater importance than the agent | The agent is invariably unknown to the author | It is the sole grammatical form permitted | It renders the report considerably shorter
exam: Select the modal verb expressing the lowest degree of certainty. | might | will | must | shall
